# The single-thread deficit, re-profiled on the composed tree — and what moved

date: 2026-08-08
host: Apple M4 Pro (8 P-core + 4 E-core = 12 logical), macOS 26.5.2, aarch64
rustc: stable, release (lto = "fat", codegen-units = 1), DEFAULT features
       (bitdepth_8 + bitdepth_16, CHECKED DisjointMut). No `-C target-cpu=native`.
base: `verify/compose-2` @ 2e0ea4d (PR #454), 766/766 dav1d-test-data.
branch: `perf/st1-kernels`, in the sibling worktree `rav1d-safe--st1`.
reference: dav1d 1.5.4 (homebrew), `--framedelay 1` (tile threading only — the
           model we implement).
vectors: `~/tmp/rav1d-perf/vec/v4k_8tile{,_10b}.avif`, 3840x2160, 4 tile cols x
         2 tile rows; dav1d decodes the matching `.ivf` from
         `~/tmp/recon-yard/vec/`, the same key frame repeated, so both sides do
         identical work per frame.

NO `nice` ON ANY TIMED OR PROFILED RUN. Darwin maps a positive nice value to
background QoS and distorts wall clock by ~40x. `nice -n 19` is for BUILDS and
for the correctness sweep only.

Every A/B below is `scripts/perf/st1_ab.sh`: two staged binaries built from
separate `cargo build` invocations, run back to back inside each round with the
arm order rotated, 5 rounds, MEDIAN OF 5, on an idle box behind a >25%-CPU
foreign-process guard that excludes the arms themselves. Raw:
`benchmarks/st1_ab_2026-08-08.tsv` (experiment, round, arm, ms/frame, frame md5,
foreign count) — the frame md5 is in every row, so each A/B doubles as its own
bit-identity check.

---

## 1. Profile first — nobody had profiled t=1 on this tree

`scripts/perf/prof_sample.sh` (`/usr/bin/sample`, 60 s of steady state, 200
decode iterations), self time per symbol via `scripts/perf/sample_selftime.py`.
46,019 leaf samples at 8bpc and 46,108 at 10bpc. New tool this session:
`scripts/perf/sample_callers.py`, which credits a subsystem's samples to the
nearest NON-matching ancestor, so "who asked for this borrow" is answerable
without reading the tree by hand.

Bucketed, converted to ms/frame at the measured wall clock (401.0 / 438.1):

    subsystem                        8bpc          10bpc      dav1d 8bpc
    ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    entropy (decode_coefs + msac
      + read_golomb + ctx)         218.3 ms       216.9 ms      ~215 ms
    DisjointMut borrow tracker      96.8 ms        89.1 ms         0 ms
    everything else (pixels)        85.9 ms       132.1 ms       ~31 ms
    ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    TOTAL                          401.0 ms       438.1 ms       246.4 ms

* **Entropy is at parity and stays at parity.** 218.3 vs dav1d's ~215 ms (the
  dav1d figure is the symbol-resolved measurement on record in
  `benchmarks/p2_kernel_profile_2026-08-07.meta`, which this profile's own
  8bpc column reproduces to within 3%). Half of our single-thread time is in a
  subsystem with no headroom. To reach 1.3x = 320 ms at 8bpc, the other
  182.7 ms has to become 102 ms.
* **The borrow tracker is the largest single addressable item and it is bigger
  than every pixel kernel combined at 8bpc** (96.8 vs 85.9 ms).
* 10bpc's extra 37 ms over 8bpc is almost entirely pixel kernels (132.1 vs
  85.9), which is what makes its ratio worse (1.74x vs 1.63x).

### Where the tracker traffic comes from (8bpc, self time by caller)

     3.92%  WithOffset::block_mut            <- itx dispatch
     3.32%  WithOffset::narrow_guard         <- itx dispatch + intra edges
     2.59%  <itx::itxfm::Fn>::call
     2.35%  recon::rav1d_recon_b_intra
     1.91%  decode::decode_b
     1.79%  ipred_prepare::rav1d_prepare_intra_edges
     1.75%  loopfilter::LfBlock::open
     1.53%  ctx::CaseSetter::set_disjoint (four instantiations)
     0.96%  cdef_arm::cdef_filter_block_8bpc_inner
     0.87%  decode::decode_sb
    ------
    24.14%  total

### NEW: the all-shards WIDE path is live at t=1 and was invisible before

`add_wide` + `remove_wide` are **10.17% of whole-decode wall at 8bpc**
(4,678 samples = 41 ms/frame). `add_wide` locks EVERY active shard (32 in a
serial decode) and scans every one, and `remove_wide` locks them all again.

Chain, from `sample_callers.py`:

    itxfm::Fn::call / prepare_intra_edges
      -> WithOffset::block_mut | narrow_guard   7.54%
        -> BorrowTracker::add_multi             7.12%
          -> add_wide / remove_wide             6.04%

The mechanism is the guard EXTENT. `narrow_guard(w, h)` reserves
`(h-1)*stride + w` pixels — an 8x8 transform block at a 3840-byte 4K stride is
26,888 bytes, which at the serial `BLOCK_SHIFT` of 12 is 7 tracker blocks on 7
distinct shards, over `MAX_SHARDS_PER_BORROW = 4`, so it promotes. Only 4x4
(3 blocks) stays narrow.

Every tracker profile on record was taken at **t = 8**, where
`tile_threading_active()` sends `block_mut` down the per-row path and the wide
list is provably empty — `benchmarks/tracker_blockshift_2026-08-08.meta` §3
records "Wide-path promotions: ZERO at every shift, on both bit depths". That
is a t=8 statement. At t=1 the wide path is the hot path.

### 10bpc pays a conversion 8bpc does not

`DisjointMut<PicBuf>::slice_as::<u16>` (2.04% self) and `mut_slice_as::<u16>`
(1.94%) together are 17.4 ms/frame at 10bpc and do not appear at all at 8bpc,
where `BD::Pixel` is `u8` and the cast is the identity. That is the
`cast_slice` + `check_cast_slice_len` half of every 16bpc pixel borrow.

---

## 2. What landed

### NEON chroma-from-luma AC (0923f5a)

`src/ipred.rs::cfl_ac_rust` was the last fully scalar per-pixel loop in the
intra path on aarch64: 2.08% of whole-decode wall at 8bpc, 1.86% at 10bpc, of
which only 0.26% was tracker — arithmetic, not bookkeeping. Six `#[arcane]` row
kernels (4:2:0 / 4:2:2 / 4:4:4 x 8bpc / 16bpc) plus a vectorised DC-removal
tail, in `src/safe_simd/ipred_arm.rs`, wired through `cfl_ac_direct` beside the
existing x86 arm.

    vector             t     before    after    ratio
    ---------------------------------------------------
    v4k_8tile          1      399.6    396.4    0.9921
    v4k_8tile_10b      1      435.9    431.4    0.9895

Bit-identity is structural, not empirical: a subsampled sum is at most 4 pixels
and the shift is at most 3, so the product tops out at `8 * (2^bd - 1)` =
2,040 / 8,184 / 32,760 at 8 / 10 / 12 bpc and never truncates in `i16` at any
depth AV1 defines; the DC accumulator is `i32` over at most 1,024 such values;
and `i32` addition is associative, so `vpadalq_s16`'s pairwise-widening order
gives the reference's sequential total exactly.

The guard shape was deliberately left alone (one immutable guard per source
row, exactly as the reference), so this is an arithmetic A/B and nothing else.

**Variant checklist — every `cfl_ac` shape, and its state:**

    layout          depth    kernel                    state
    ----------------------------------------------------------------------
    4:2:0           8bpc     ac_row_420_8bpc           NEON  (vpaddlq_u8)
    4:2:0           16bpc    ac_row_420_16bpc          NEON  (vpaddq_u16)
    4:2:2           8bpc     ac_row_422_8bpc           NEON
    4:2:2           16bpc    ac_row_422_16bpc          NEON
    4:4:4           8bpc     ac_row_444_8bpc           NEON  (vmovl_u8)
    4:4:4           16bpc    ac_row_444_16bpc          NEON
    DC removal      both     ac_remove_dc              NEON  (vpadalq_s16)
    ----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ss_ver && !ss_hor  --    (4:4:0)                   REFUSED -> reference
    right-edge column pad    --                        scalar (replication)
    bottom-edge row pad      --                        scalar (copy_from_slice)
    `active_w % 8` tail      --                        scalar (0-3 pixels)

AV1 defines no 4:4:0 chroma layout, and the reference derives its shift from
`!ss_ver`/`!ss_hor` on that assumption, so the dispatch returns `false` for it
rather than guessing — the scalar reference stays the oracle for a shape
neither of us can produce. The three padding/tail items are replication and
memcpy, not arithmetic, and stayed as the reference writes them so the
byte-for-byte equality argument covers them trivially.

**Correctness: full corpus, set-diff BY NAME with the actual MD5 in the key.**
`examples/md5_inventory` over all 768 dav1d-test-data vectors, joined on
`(group, name)` against `benchmarks/aarch64_md5_fixes_2026-08-07_final.tsv.zst`:
768 rows both sides, 0 only-in-ref, 0 only-in-new, **0 differing
(status, actual-md5) pairs**, 766 PASS / 2 SKIP either side. Plus frame md5
equality on all 7 local vectors (8- and 10-bit, 1- and 8-tile, 4K / 1024 / 256)
and in every one of the 20 A/B cells.

---

## 3. Honest negatives — three shapes tried, three did not ship

### 3a. Per-row guards for the 8bpc itx: +2.4%, REVERTED

Replacing `block_mut`'s one wide guard in the thirteen 8bpc arms of
`itx_arm::itxfm_add_dispatch` with a compact `w x h` round trip through per-row
guards (`compact_read_per_row` + `compact_write_back_per_row`, i.e. exactly the
tile-threading path, unconditionally):

    v4k_8tile t=1   base 385.5   rowguard 394.7   ratio 1.0239   md5 identical

The wide registration is expensive but it is ONE registration; `2h` narrow ones
plus `2wh` bytes of memcpy cost more. Not the direction.

### 3b. One wide guard for the 16bpc itx: +4.7%, REVERTED

The mirror image. `itx_arm`'s 16bpc arm writes the picture through one narrow
guard PER ROW with no copy; collapsing that to a single `strided_slice_mut`
over the block (branching on `tile_threading_active()` so threading kept the
per-row form):

    v4k_8tile_10b t=1   base 446.8   wide 468.0   ratio 1.0474   md5 identical

**Taken together, 3a and 3b are the rule:** with no copy involved, `h` narrow
registrations BEAT one wide one; the 8bpc loss was the memcpy, not the guard
count. Both shipped shapes are therefore already the better of the two, and
`cdef_arm`, `ipred::splat_dc`, `angular_ipred` and `loopfilter::LfBlock` were
audited and are all already on per-row narrow guards. **The guard-shape lever
is exhausted from `src/`.**

### 3c. Thread-local scratch for the 16bpc itx `tmp`: null

Every 16bpc transform allocated a fresh `[0i32; 256]` — 1 KiB that safe Rust
must initialise, for a 4x4 that uses 16 slots. Carrying one buffer per thread
in a `thread_local! { RefCell<..> }` instead (byte-identical: the row pass fully
writes `tmp[..w*h]` before anything reads it):

    v4k_8tile_10b t=1   431.4 -> 430.9   ratio 0.9990   md5 identical

Inside the round-to-round spread. Reverted rather than carry a `RefCell` borrow
on the hot path for nothing.

### 3d. 16bpc transforms above 16x16 are NOT worth porting on this vector

The brief flagged 32/64-point 16bpc transforms and `WHT_WHT` as unported
(`itx_arm_hbd::MAXDIM = 16`), and the 16bpc-itx vein as productive. Measured
before writing any kernel: an arm with `#[inline(never)]` on
`inv_txfm_add_rust`, so the scalar reference monomorphises to one symbol per
`(W, H)` in the profile instead of inlining into the dispatch. On
`v4k_8tile_10b` at t=1, **no `inv_txfm_add_rust` symbol appears anywhere in the
profile** — the scalar fallback is under the sampling floor. `itxfm::Fn::call`'s
3.55% of self time is the inlined NEON driver and `add_row`, not unported
sizes. A 32-point port would have bought nothing here. (This says nothing about
a vector whose transform mix actually uses those sizes; it says the 4K still we
tune against does not.)

---

## 4. For the tracker owner — one measured knob, and it points opposite ways

`BLOCK_SHIFT` is fixed at 12 for a serial decode; `block_shift_for()` adapts
only once `set_parallelism` has declared more than one thread. Building the
same source with `--features blockshift-adaptive` (which forces adaptation at
t=1 too, and is the ONLY difference between the arms — no source edit, so the
tracker crate is untouched):

    vector             t     base   adaptive   ratio
    ----------------------------------------------------
    v4k_8tile          1    397.8      383.9   0.9651   <- -3.5%
    v4k_8tile_10b      1    438.7      464.4   1.0586   <- +5.9%

Both bit-identical. The 8bpc win is the wide-path escalation of §1
disappearing: at shift 14 a 4K luma plane gets ~4.3 picture rows per tracker
block, so a strided transform block spans 2 blocks instead of 15 and takes the
single-shard fast path with no copy. The 10bpc loss is the same coarsening seen
from the other side: that path already uses per-row narrow borrows, and a
bigger block funnels more of them onto one shard (longer overlap scans, more
slot pressure).

This reproduces, with n=5 and an idle box, the direction of the n=1 row in
`benchmarks/tracker_blockshift_2026-08-08.meta` §5b (base 404.0 / adapt 384.6
at 8bpc t=1; base 510.9 / adapt 528.9 at 10bpc t=1), which that campaign could
not resolve and therefore excluded from the shipped default. It is now
resolved, and it is a genuine bit-depth-dependent trade, not noise.

Two things follow, neither of which is `src/`'s to make:

1. A serial `BLOCK_SHIFT` keyed on the pixel stride (or simply on `BD::BPC`)
   rather than on the buffer length would take the 8bpc win without the 10bpc
   loss. `block_shift_for` sees only `len`, and a 10-bit plane is both twice the
   bytes and twice the stride, so length alone cannot separate them.
2. `MAX_SHARDS_PER_BORROW = 4` with `shard_of(block) = block & mask` is what
   turns any strided block borrow into a wide one at 4K. A shard mapping that
   kept a short run of consecutive blocks on one shard would keep those borrows
   on the multi-shard fast path at any block shift.

---

## 5. Where t=1 stands now

`scripts/perf/verify_gap.sh`, 5 rounds, arms rotated, every cell re-run until it
commits with zero foreign processes over 25% CPU; each stream timed at 2 and 20
frames with `total = alpha + beta*frames` fitted so process startup drops out.
Raw: `benchmarks/st1_gap_2026-08-08.tsv`.

See the table appended below (`## 5b`), written after the sweep completed.

## 6. What this says about the 1.3x target

At 8bpc, 218 of 401 ms is entropy at parity with dav1d. The reachable 183 ms is
97 ms of borrow tracker and 86 ms of pixel kernels, and after this session no
single pixel kernel is above 21 ms (the loop-filter family) — the four largest
after that are the recon driver's own loop (14), CDEF (7), the intra
predictors (7) and the transforms (8). **Kernel work alone cannot reach 1.3x at
t=1**: zeroing every pixel kernel leaves 315 ms, which is 1.28x, and that is the
ceiling of this lane. The tracker is the binding constraint, and §4 is the
cheapest measured lever into it.
